== Few-Shot Segmentation

Few-shot segmentation aims to segment novel object classes given only a handful of annotated examples, typically one or five support images with ground-truth masks. The foundational paradigm traces to Prototypical Networks @snell2017prototypical, which learn a metric space in which class prototypes are computed as the mean of support embeddings and query pixels are classified by distance to these prototypes. PANet @wang2019panet extended this idea with prototype alignment regularization and masked average pooling, enforcing that the learned prototypes remain consistent when the roles of support and query are reversed. PFENet @tian2022pfenet introduced training-agnostic spatial priors derived from high-level backbone features, enriching query representations without requiring episodic fine-tuning. HSNet @min2021hsnet constructed dense 4D hypercorrelation tensors from multi-level feature similarities between support and query images, then compressed them through center-pivot convolutions to produce accurate correspondence maps. VAT @hong2022vat replaced the convolutional aggregation in HSNet with a 4D Swin Transformer over cost volumes, capturing longer-range dependencies in the correlation space. BAM @lang2022bam addressed the often-overlooked problem of base-class distractors in the query by learning _what not to segment_, suppressing regions that belong to previously seen classes.

These methods share a common design pattern. They build task-specific encoder-decoder architectures trained episodically on support-query pairs, learning segmentation largely from scratch on limited supervision. A recent line of work instead leverages SAM's pre-trained segmentation capability and focuses on translating visual references into SAM-compatible prompts. VRP-SAM @sun2024vrpsam introduces a Visual Reference Prompt encoder that maps a support image-mask pair into prompt embeddings for SAM's decoder, achieving competitive few-shot performance without modifying SAM's weights. ProtoSAM @ayzenberg2025protosam combines DINOv2 feature extraction with prototypical matching to identify the target region in the query, then feeds the resulting coarse localization to SAM as spatial prompts for final mask generation. Both VRP-SAM and ProtoSAM generate prompts in a _single forward pass_, treating prompt construction as a feed-forward prediction problem.

Our approach departs from this single-pass paradigm by learning an _iterative_ prompt policy through reinforcement learning. Rather than producing a fixed set of prompts from the support example, the agent observes SAM's intermediate mask output at each step and selects corrective point prompts that progressively refine the segmentation. This closed-loop interaction with SAM's predictions enables the policy to recover from poor initial prompts and adapt to complex object geometries, distinguishing our method from all prior few-shot and one-shot SAM approaches including those evaluated on FSS-1000 @li2020fss.
